    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Project KO11 Is Proof That Geely Wants To \"Knock Out\" Range Rover, Launches Next Year in Europe\nVolkswagen's Steering Rack Bolt Recall Includes 239,202 Atlas and Tiguan Sport Utility Vehicles\n26 photos\nPhoto: Volkswagen\nWhile road salt may be great for keeping winter roads drivable, sodium chloride and moisture are a terrible cocktail for a bunch of stuff underneath your car. The Volkswagen Group knows it all too well due to a bolt that secures the steering rack to the front subframe, namely a bolt that can prematurely corrode from salt and water exposure.\nThe worldwide recall now numbers more than four million vehicles, of which the Atlas and Tiguan are listed with 239,202 units by the Kraftfahrt-Bundesamt. As per Germany's Federal Motor Transport Authority, the subject bolt can easily break, leaving the driver without steering control. The KBA presently monitors the problem under campaign number 17124R.\nAs far as the Atlas people mover and Tiguan crossover are concerned, suspect vehicles were produced between November 28, 2016, and September 22, 2018. The Volkswagen Group calls this campaign 48VT, and the KBA lists precisely 454 affected vehicles in the German market.\nAs noted earlier, corrosion is the biggest problem here. Over time, road salt exposure and other environmental factors can rust the steering rack mounting bolt so much that a failure cannot be ruled out. When it breaks, the steering rack's housing can also fracture, leading to a loss of steering control at high speed.\nVolkswagen's US subsidiary describes the same failure mode for the US market, and the recall filing published by the NHTSA reveals that a replacement bolt with better corrosion resistance is on the menu. The Wolfsburg-based automaker claims the new part uses a polymer coating designed specifically to keep salt and moisture away from the fastener.\nPhoto: Volkswagen The question is, was this a supplier issue or simply Volkswagen opting for a cheaper part to save production costs? While that question may not have an answer right now, it doesn't change the fact that over four million cars and sport utility vehicles are being recalled across the world.\nIn the US, campaign 26V590 also includes a bunch of 2019 through 2021 model year Audi Q3 vehicles. Said campaign uses 48VT for the Volkswagen side of the recall and 48LG for the Audi side. In both cases, owner notification letters are scheduled for November 2026.\nThe KBA's campaign is both narrower and broader at the same time. Not only does it cover more vehicles than the US campaign, but it doesn't include the Audi Q3. Regardless, the recall includes MQB-based vehicles exclusively. The group found this problem through multiple investigations into steering rack bolt corrosion reports across multiple nameplates.\nVolkswagen hasn't announced a completion date for the repairs beyond the notification schedule, which is only natural when you remember how many replacement bolts are required worldwide. That said, both the Atlas and the Tiguan are the most popular Volkswagen crossovers in the US market.\nPer the Q3 2026 sales release from Volkswagen of America, the Tiguan leads with 28,091 deliveries for the quarter. The Atlas and Atlas Cross Sport moved 15,375 and 6,889 units during the analyzed three-month period.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolkswagen Tiguan Volkswagen Atlas Volkswagen Atlas Cross Sport Volkswagen recall\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 5 Fascinating Cars That Wowed the Crowds 40 Years Ago at the Paris Motor Show",
